<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austrian People's Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ÖVP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austrian People's Party (ÖVP) is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s main Christian democratic and conservative party, founded in April 1945 as the successor of the interwar Christian Social Party. It governed in grand coalitions with the Social Democrats for long periods after 1945 and has supplied many chancellors, from Leopold Figl to Sebastian Kurz. It is organised as a federation of leagues for farmers, business people and employees.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austrian-peoples-party.jpg" alt="Austrian People's Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThecentreCZ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Austrian_People%27s_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party is an unofficial successor of the Christian Social Party, a staunchly conservative movement founded in 1893 by Karl Lueger, the mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Most of the founding members of the new party had belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-bulgaria">Fatherland Front</a>, led by Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a>, himself a member of the Christian Social Party before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a>. Some in the party still honour Dollfuss for resisting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, but the regime he built was authoritarian and has been called Austrofascism. The ÖVP was established immediately after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s independence was restored in 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the first postwar election, in 1945, the ÖVP won almost half the vote and an absolute majority in the legislature. Memories of the hyper-partisanship of the First Republic led it to keep the grand coalition with the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the Social Democratic Party of Austria that had governed since early 1945. It remained the senior partner in coalition with the Social Democratic Party of Austria until 1966 and governed alone from 1966 to 1970. It returned to government in 1986, and has not been wholly out of power since 1945, owing to a long tradition that all major interest groups were to be consulted on policy.</p><p>After the 1999 election the party formed a coalition with the Freedom Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> under Jörg Haider, and Wolfgang Schüssel became Chancellor, the first from the ÖVP since 1970. This caused widespread outrage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> imposed informal diplomatic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> on Austria, the first it had imposed on a member state; they were dropped a few months later after a fact-finding mission by three former European prime ministers, the so-called three wise men. In the 2002 election the ÖVP won 42.27% of the vote. In 2006 it became the junior partner in a grand coalition under Alfred Gusenbauer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party is described as Christian democratic, conservative and liberal-conservative, a catch-all party of the centre-right. For most of its existence it defined itself as Catholic and anti-socialist, holding to subsidiarity as set out in the encyclical Quadragesimo anno and to decentralisation. In the first postwar election it presented itself as the Austrian Party and the Party of the centre, and its economic policy generally upheld a social market economy. Its strongholds have long been the rural states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a>, while it is less popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgenland">Burgenland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. With the Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> it has been one of Austria's two traditional major parties.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Austrian People's Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politics was long governed by Proporz: important posts were shared between the Social Democratic Party (SPÖ) and the <b>Austrian People's Party</b> (ÖVP), and the chambers of interest groups were consulted on nearly all legislation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
